  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════ SECTION 4: RULES ═══════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="section-card">
    <div class="section-header">
      <div class="section-number-badge">4</div>
      <div class="section-header-text">
        <div class="section-phase-label">Phase 4 · Constraint Engine</div>
        <h2 class="section-title">Cinematic Rules — Agent Must Apply</h2>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="section-body">
      <div class="rules-list">

        <div class="rule-block">
          <div class="rule-title">
            <span class="rule-tag">RULE 01</span>
            Natural Light Priority
          </div>
          <div class="rule-body">Always lead with natural light. Analyze brightness distribution across each image and identify the source direction. <strong>Prioritize images where natural light is the dominant illumination source.</strong> Artificially overlit or flash-heavy images must be deprioritized and placed only when no better alternative exists.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="rule-block">
          <div class="rule-title">
            <span class="rule-tag">RULE 02</span>
            The 3-Image Rule
          </div>
          <div class="rule-body">Use <strong>no more than 3 images per room</strong> in the final sequence unless the room is classified as Kitchen or Master Suite, in which case a maximum of <strong>5 images</strong> is permitted. Exceeding these limits dilutes pacing and reduces emotional impact.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="rule-block">
          <div class="rule-title">
            <span class="rule-tag">RULE 03</span>
            Hero Room Screen Time
          </div>
          <div class="rule-body">Hero rooms — <strong>Living Room, Kitchen, and Master Bedroom</strong> — must receive the greatest screen time allocation in the final video. These three rooms collectively represent the primary purchase drivers for the majority of buyers. Never compress hero room sequences to accommodate secondary rooms.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="rule-block">
          <div class="rule-title">
            <span class="rule-tag">RULE 04</span>
            No Back-to-Back Room Repeat
          </div>
          <div class="rule-body">Never place two images of the same room type consecutively unless they are part of an intentional multi-shot sequence within the same room block. <strong>Never sequence bedroom → bedroom</strong> across different rooms. Use hallway, staircase, or transitional images as buffers where available.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="rule-block">
          <div class="rule-title">
            <span class="rule-tag">RULE 05</span>
            Exterior Closure Mandate
          </div>
          <div class="rule-body">The final image in all outputs <strong>must always be an exterior or aerial shot.</strong> Ending on an interior image closes the viewer's mental journey inward. Ending on an exterior provides emotional closure, geographic anchoring, and a lasting positive brand impression of the property.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="rule-block flag-rule">
          <div class="rule-title">
            <span class="rule-tag flag-tag">FLAG</span>
            Low Priority Image Detection
          </div>
          <div class="rule-body">Automatically flag any image identified as: <em>blurry</em> (edge sharpness score below threshold), <em>poorly lit</em> (luma average below 0.35), <em>cluttered</em> (object density anomaly detected), or <em>contains visible people</em> (human detection confidence above 0.70). Flagged images are classified as <em>LOW PRIORITY</em> and must not appear in positions 1–3 or 11–12 of the final sequence.</div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

